WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.360
With century's worth of history, there’s&nbsp;
been plenty of time for sitcoms to establish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.600
long-standing records within the genre.&nbsp;
Although records were meant to be broken,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.738
some have stood the test of time. Let’s take a&nbsp;
look at the sitcom records that may stand forever.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.360
Appropriately for a country that&nbsp;
aired the first sitcom on TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.120
Britain can also claim the&nbsp;
longest-running sitcom in history.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.600
In January 1973, BBC One&nbsp;
aired the first episode of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:32.560
"Last of the Summer Wine," a Roy Clarke&nbsp;
sitcom about three older men who engage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:37.040
in a variety of youthful hijinks and&nbsp;
adventures in the absence of spouses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.480
jobs, or other responsibilities. The first&nbsp;
season of the show wasn't very well-received.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.880
“Admit it. You’re bored already.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.120
Nevertheless, "Last of the Summer&nbsp;
Wine," like its characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.920
got better with age, and eventually became&nbsp;
an institution of British entertainment.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.960
As time went on, it seemed impossible to kill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:03.280
surviving the deaths and departures of major cast&nbsp;
members and the growing indifference of the BBC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:08.640
It even continued during the changing tide of&nbsp;
public opinion as the program entered its second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:14.640
third, and fourth decades. But in 2010, "Last&nbsp;
of the Summer Wine" finally went off the air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.280
setting the record officially at 37&nbsp;
years, seven months, and 25 days.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.720
Despite this admittedly impressive accomplishment,&nbsp;
it should be mentioned that "Last of the Summer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:31.200
Wine" doesn't entirely deserve its place on this&nbsp;
list. Why? Well, because this record will almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.720
certainly be broken. Specifically, by a certain&nbsp;
decades-spanning-primetime-cartoon, which will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:42.401
leave "Summer Wine" with the consolation prize&nbsp;
of being the longest-running live action sitcom.

00:01:42.401 --> 00:01:45.440
The show that will likely unseat "The Last of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.560
the Summer Wine" in terms of sheer longevity&nbsp;
already holds numerous sitcom records.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.840
"The Simpsons" was officially launched&nbsp;
in 1989, and it's been going ever since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.480
becoming the longest-running American sitcom&nbsp;
and the longest-running scripted primetime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:06.960
series of any kind. When it was renewed for&nbsp;
its 33rd and 34th seasons in March 2021, "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:13.360
Simpsons" ensured that its total episode count&nbsp;
would be at least 757. If we’re being honest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:19.200
it’ll probably be a lot more — far and away&nbsp;
the most episodes for any animated sitcom.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:25.440
That renewal also meant that "The Simpsons" will&nbsp;
be on the air at least until the end of 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.680
at which point it will be less than four&nbsp;
years away from the sitcom longevity record&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.640
held by "Summer Wine." There's no end in&nbsp;
sight for Bart, Lisa, Homer, and Marge.&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:40.800
After its longtime network distributor, 21st&nbsp;
Century Fox, was purchased by Disney in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.400
"The Simpsons" arrived on the Disney+ streaming&nbsp;
service. Here, it will presumably remain until it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.960
stops making a profit. Given the way Disney&nbsp;
tends to treat its intellectual property,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.440
it's extremely unlikely the show will&nbsp;
be canceled prior to its 38th year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.680
"The Simpsons" just keeps going, smashing&nbsp;
record after record in the process.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.960
We are by no means done talking about&nbsp;
"The Simpsons," which, among other things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:09.680
is famous for its guest stars. A hallmark&nbsp;
of the show has been the way celebrities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:14.560
appear as themselves. It’s a star-studded&nbsp;
roster that includes, just to name a few,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:20.400
Leonard Nimoy, Rachel Maddow, Lady Gaga, and Hugh&nbsp;
Hefner. There was a time, in the show's heyday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.840
when you weren't really a celebrity&nbsp;
if you hadn't been on "The Simpsons."

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.600
With that in mind, it's no surprise that&nbsp;
"The Simpsons" hold the Guinness world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:32.640
record for most guest stars in an animated&nbsp;
series of any kind. According to Guinness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:39.440
the total number was 810 as of April 2020,&nbsp;
though obviously the number is even higher now.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:45.040
It would be utterly inconceivable to imagine&nbsp;
another show, let alone another animated show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:50.640
being as guest star-friendly as "The Simpsons".&nbsp;
And, as previously mentioned, "The Simpsons" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:56.720
not going away any time soon. Season 33 hasn't&nbsp;
even arrived yet and we already know that comedian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:02.560
Maurice LaMarche and "NFL Redzone" host Scott&nbsp;
Hanson will be added to the ranks. So, it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:08.080
pretty safe to say that no TV program will likely&nbsp;
ever have more guest stars than "The Simpsons."

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.000
As you might expect, a show with the lifespan and&nbsp;
accolades of "The Simpsons" also has a history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.760
of cleaning up at the Emmy Awards, the highest&nbsp;
honor an American television program can achieve.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.760
It won its first Emmy in 1990 for&nbsp;
the episode "Life on the Fast Lane,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.000
and would go on to absolutely crush&nbsp;
the awards for the rest of the decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.600
including stellar individual years like 1992,&nbsp;
in which it tallied six wins for Outstanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:38.800
Voice-Over Performance, and 1998, when it&nbsp;
won Emmys in three separate categories.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:42.160
While it never saw that particular&nbsp;
level of dominance again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:47.520
"The Simpsons" continued to rack up Emmys&nbsp;
throughout its existence, most recently in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:53.360
when it won for an episode addressing themes of&nbsp;
homophobic bigotry called "Mad About the Toy." It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:58.560
was the 11th "Simpsons" victory in the category&nbsp;
"Outstanding Animated Program." No other show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:04.720
has won in that category more than five times.&nbsp;
Similarly, Hank Azaria and Dan Castellaneta have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:09.760
each won four Emmys for Outstanding Voice-Over&nbsp;
Performance for their work on "The Simpsons."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.440
At this level, they’re tied for most&nbsp;
ever, alongside Seth MacFarlane.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:19.840
All told, "The Simpsons" has set a Guinness&nbsp;
world record by amassing 34 total Emmy Awards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:24.720
the fourth-most of any show in history. No&nbsp;
other animated series is even in the top ten.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:30.240
Given the unlikelihood of any other animated show&nbsp;
becoming a cultural monolith like "The Simpsons,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.560
it seems safe to say that this "Simpsons"&nbsp;
record will also never be broken.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.960
Now that we're done celebrating "The&nbsp;
Simpsons," let's talk about some Emmy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:45.360
Award records set by non-animated shows. First&nbsp;
up: "30 Rock," Tina Fey's lovingly irreverent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:52.480
"business of television" sitcom that aired&nbsp;
on and made fun of NBC from 2006 to 2013.

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:57.760
Despite consistently shaky ratings, "30 Rock"&nbsp;
was beloved for its lampooning of the backstage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:03.360
workings of "Saturday Night Live," and the NBC&nbsp;
network in general. So it was appropriate when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:09.920
Fey's "SNL" parody of vice presidential candidate&nbsp;
Sarah Palin in 2008 led to an increase in exposure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:16.320
and appreciation for "30 Rock." This culminated&nbsp;
at the 2009 Emmy Awards, in which the show's third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:22.080
season set the Guinness world record for most Emmy&nbsp;
nominations for a comedy show in a single year.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:26.480
In addition to "Outstanding Comedy Series,"&nbsp;
which it won for the third year in a row,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:31.200
Fey and Alec Baldwin were both nominated for&nbsp;
acting, along with eight other performers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.920
in guest and supporting roles. Ironically, Fey&nbsp;
would win an Emmy that year not for "30 Rock,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:41.440
but for her send-up of Palin. With three more&nbsp;
nominations for direction, four for writing, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:48.240
four in the creative categories, "30 Rock" Season&nbsp;
3 garnered 22 Emmy nominations. It’s a number that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:52.960
hasn't been surpassed by a comedy show in more&nbsp;
than ten years, and probably won't be at all.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.240
The record for most Emmy victories for a&nbsp;
comedy in a single season is held by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:04.400
most modern show on this list. No comedy has&nbsp;
ever done what "Schitt's Creek" did in 2020.

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:10.240
Coming off its final season, the Canadian&nbsp;
sitcom won all seven of the major comedy awards:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:16.320
directing, writing, the four acting awards, and&nbsp;
Outstanding Comedy Series. This is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:22.080
no comedy in history has accomplished. Dan Levy,&nbsp;
who worked on "Schitt's Creek" in every capacity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:27.600
became the first person in history to win an Emmy&nbsp;
for acting, writing, directing, and producing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.960
the same year. Throw in a couple of creative&nbsp;
category wins and "Schitt's Creek" took home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:38.960
nine Emmys, a world record for a comedy in a&nbsp;
single season. It did all of this while up against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:44.000
the finales of beloved sitcoms like "Modern&nbsp;
Family" and "The Good Place." Not to mention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:48.800
"The Marvelous Mrs. Maisel," which had previously&nbsp;
held the record for most single-season wins.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:53.040
How did the "Schitt's Creek" sweep&nbsp;
happen, especially considering it had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:58.240
never won a single Emmy in its five previous&nbsp;
seasons? Some have speculated that voters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:00.880
quarantined in their homes due to COVID-19,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:05.920
had spent their time binging "Schitt's Creek" on&nbsp;
Netflix, leading to a sudden swell of support for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:10.800
the show. It was a unique situation that&nbsp;
we're unlikely to ever see replicated.

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:16.240
As we've seen, there are several sitcoms over the&nbsp;
years that have had a positive relationship with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:21.760
the Emmy Awards. Still, the one with the most&nbsp;
total Emmy wins hasn't even been mentioned yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:27.280
In 1993, "Cheers," one of the most&nbsp;
recognizable and popular sitcoms of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:32.480
went off the air after 11 seasons&nbsp;
and 28 Emmy Awards. But in an almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:38.320
unprecedented turn of events, the "Cheers"&nbsp;
spin-off was arguably even more successful.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:39.920
“Guilty as charged.”

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:45.440
"Frasier" followed psychologist Frasier Crane,&nbsp;
who had joined the cast of "Cheers" in Season 3.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:49.840
Frasier hosted a Seattle radio program and&nbsp;
interacted with a delightfully eccentric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:54.960
group of family and friends. The show was a hit&nbsp;
at the Emmys from the start. It won the award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.960 --> 00:09:00.160
for Outstanding Comedy Series after its first&nbsp;
season and would go on to win for Seasons 2-5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:05.360
making it the first series to win&nbsp;
that award in five consecutive years.

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:10.320
By the time "Frasier" was finished with its&nbsp;
own 11-season run, it had racked up a total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:16.320
of 37 Emmys, which was, at the time, the&nbsp;
record for a scripted series of any kind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:21.600
It has since been eclipsed in that regard by "Game&nbsp;
of Thrones," but "Frasier" still holds the record&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:27.680
for sitcoms. "The Simpsons" is the only show that&nbsp;
has even a vaguely realistic shot at beating it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:31.200
Nothing else has even come close,&nbsp;
though "Modern Family" did manage to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.960
tie the "five consecutive Outstanding&nbsp;
Comedy Series wins" accomplishment.

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:40.480
"Frasier" was anchored by the performance of&nbsp;
its leading man, Kelsey Grammer, who won a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:45.680
record-tying four Emmys for his portrayal of the&nbsp;
title character. He was also nominated for playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:51.520
Frasier Crane in both "Cheers" and "Wings." For&nbsp;
a time, he also commanded the highest salary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:57.920
in network television, at one point signing a&nbsp;
deal with NBC worth $1.6 million per episode.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:02.720
That record, however, was almost immediately&nbsp;
broken by Ray Romano for the show "Everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:09.680
Loves Raymond," for which Romano got paid $1.8&nbsp;
million per episode by CBS. And a few years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:14.800
the same network gave even more to Charlie&nbsp;
Sheen for his work on "Two and a Half Men."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:20.080
While Sheen's contract is technically still the&nbsp;
most lucrative per-episode deal in sitcom history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:25.120
context has to be considered. He was only making&nbsp;
that amount of money for the show's eighth season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:30.320
and the last four episodes of that season were&nbsp;
canceled after Sheen's explosive falling out with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:37.840
CBS. Likewise, Romano's $1.8 million was only for&nbsp;
one season, whereas Grammer's deal was for two.

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:42.080
When you do the math, the $76 million&nbsp;
Grammer received for the last two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:46.800
seasons of "Frasier" shakes out to a bit more&nbsp;
than what Romano got paid during his most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:51.920
lucrative contract. However, it should be noted&nbsp;
that Romano also earned royalties for the show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:57.760
Regardless of how you define "highest-paid," no&nbsp;
sitcom actor since Sheen has come anywhere close,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.760 --> 00:10:59.840
and we'd be surprised if it ever happened again.

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:05.520
Charlie Sheen wasn't the only performer CBS&nbsp;
opened the bank vault for after the success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:09.280
of "Two and a Half Men." The show,&nbsp;
which was premised on two brothers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:14.240
Charlie and Alan Harper, raising Alan's&nbsp;
son, Jake, also holds the distinction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:18.000
employing the highest-paid child&nbsp;
actor on television of all time.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:23.440
Angus T. Jones, who played Jake,&nbsp;
reportedly made $250,000 per episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:29.040
a number that Guinness recognized in 2010 after&nbsp;
an analysis of TV earnings by the New York Post.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:33.920
A 2014 article by the Post, however, claims&nbsp;
that by the time Jones left the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:39.440
he was making considerably more —&nbsp;
$350,000 per episode. By that time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:43.280
Jones had started publicly disparaging&nbsp;
"Two and a Half Men" for conflicting with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:48.560
his religious values. The show's 10th season&nbsp;
would be his last as a regular cast member,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:53.840
with the role of "the half" being taken over&nbsp;
by Amber Tamblyn for the final two seasons.

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:57.520
Jones did, however, make a cameo&nbsp;
appearance in the series finale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:02.320
So, maybe, his being the most financially&nbsp;
secure child actor in TV history may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:06.240
occasionally overcome his distaste at the&nbsp;
sinful nature of "Two and a Half Men."

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:10.560
The earning numbers we've looked&nbsp;
at so far are all well and good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:15.040
but there's only one man who can reasonably claim&nbsp;
to be the highest-earning comedian of all time.

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:21.280
It’s been more than 23 years since the legendary&nbsp;
sitcom "Seinfeld" wrapped up its iconic nine-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:26.400
run. However, when Forbes released the 2020&nbsp;
edition of its annual list of the world's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:31.040
highest-paid celebrities, Jerry Seinfeld&nbsp;
was once again the highest ranked comedian.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:36.400
He raked in $51 million over the course of that&nbsp;
year. That was enough for Guinness, at least for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:40.960
the time, as they update that record on a yearly&nbsp;
basis. But it's also just difficult to imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:45.760
another comedian ever making more money over&nbsp;
the course of their career than Jerry Seinfeld.

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:48.880
“I don’t know how to tell you&nbsp;
this, but I’m a funny person.”

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:55.680
Seinfeld has reportedly earned as much as $400&nbsp;
million per year from "Seinfeld" syndication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.680 --> 00:13:00.800
royalties alone. He also recently signed&nbsp;
a $500 million deal with Netflix for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:05.600
rights to the show. Beyond that, though, is&nbsp;
the sheer longevity of his comedy career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:08.640
which he somehow managed to get paid to reinvent.

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:14.240
The 2002 documentary "Comedian" follows Seinfeld&nbsp;
around the comedy circuit in the years following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:19.600
the end of the show that made him famous. The doc&nbsp;
hangs on the detail that Seinfeld has decided to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:25.120
completely overhaul his act and cut out every&nbsp;
previous joke and routine he'd ever told.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:29.360
The choice paid off: Not only did&nbsp;
"Comedian" make a couple million dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:33.680
followed by his popular “Comedians in Cars&nbsp;
Getting Coffee” series, but his ongoing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:40.000
relevance convinced Netflix to pay him $20 million&nbsp;
for a new comedy special as recently as 2020.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:42.560
Just as it's difficult to imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:47.520
any comedian making more money than Jerry&nbsp;
Seinfeld, it's also difficult to imagine any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:54.640
network making more money from a single episode of&nbsp;
comedy television than NBC did on May 6th, 2004.

00:13:54.640 --> 00:14:00.400
That was the day NBC aired the series finale&nbsp;
of "Friends," another sitcom that etched itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:05.920
into the hearts and minds of a generation.&nbsp;
The show's 10-season run kicked off in 1994,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:09.760
introducing the world to Jennifer&nbsp;
Aniston, Lisa Kudrow, Courteney Cox,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:14.800
David Schwimmer, Matt LeBlanc, and Matthew&nbsp;
Perry. Each of them were reportedly making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:19.840
a million dollars per episode by the end,&nbsp;
due to the program's massive popularity.

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:24.800
When it was time to bring "Friends" to its&nbsp;
conclusion after a decade of success, it was clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:30.480
to everyone that the final double-sized episode&nbsp;
would be a must-see event for the entire country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:36.400
As a result, NBC sold advertising spots on that&nbsp;
episode at rates unheard of for anything short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:42.960
of the Super Bowl. Every 30 seconds worth of&nbsp;
ad time during those 44 minutes of TV cost two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:48.880
million dollars, which remains a Guinness world&nbsp;
record for any television series to this day. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:53.600
we've seen elsewhere on this list, the fracturing&nbsp;
of the television audience in the era of online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:58.560
media and streaming services makes it all but&nbsp;
impossible that such a feat will ever be repeated.

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:04.160
The sitcom format that most of us are familiar&nbsp;
with involves episodes airing in 30-minute time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:09.680
slots. Traditionally, it was 22 minutes of sitcom&nbsp;
interspersed with eight minutes of commercials.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:15.200
Occasionally a sitcom will run longer, taking&nbsp;
up an hour's worth of airtime or operating under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:19.760
a different format due to an alternative&nbsp;
revenue model from standard advertising.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:23.440
Still, you rarely ever see&nbsp;
sitcoms that are shorter. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:28.160
believe it or not, they do exist, and&nbsp;
some sitcoms are only a minute long.

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:33.680
In 1998 TV Land, a cable network dedicated&nbsp;
to broadcasting classic television episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:39.200
and movies, introduced "60 Second Sitcoms."&nbsp;
They were tiny, standalone mini-shows that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:44.080
appeared in between TV Land's programming and&nbsp;
its commercials. The network was brand new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:48.880
having launched in 1996, and hadn't even&nbsp;
sold ad space during its first year.

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:55.520
Shows in TV Land’s "60 Second Sitcoms" lineup&nbsp;
included "The Gaveltons," which chronicled the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.520 --> 00:16:01.520
adventures of an absurdly litigious nuclear&nbsp;
family, and "All's Well," where every episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:07.280
ended with a police officer showing up to eat&nbsp;
pie. TV Land is recognized by Guinness as having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:12.160
produced the shortest comedy show in history, and&nbsp;
we dare somebody to try and break that record.

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:18.080
Everybody Loves Raymond, and that includes the&nbsp;
greatest sitcom records today. Brad Garrett,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:21.680
who played Raymond's brother, Robert,&nbsp;
in the show “Everybody Loves Raymond”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:26.240
won three Emmy Awards for “Outstanding&nbsp;
Supporting Actor in a Comedy Series.”

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:31.760
Beyond his acting abilities and his skill at&nbsp;
poker, Garrett stands out for two reasons:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:37.520
his deep distinctive voice, and his towering&nbsp;
height. Standing 6 foot 8 inches tall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:42.880
he would eventually be recognized by Guinness&nbsp;
as history's tallest actor in a leading role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:47.760
That's not just for sitcoms. According to&nbsp;
Guinness, Garrett is the tallest leading man in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:54.000
any screen medium. He suprasses movie stars like&nbsp;
James Cromwell at 6' 7", the tallest actor to ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:59.360
receive an Oscar nomination, as well as both Tim&nbsp;
Robbins and Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson at 6' 5".

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:03.760
Sorry, "A Princess Bride" fans&nbsp;
— although Andre the Giant was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:10.880
6’11", he was hardly the lead. Same for Richard&nbsp;
Kiel, who was 7' 2" but never a leading man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:15.280
The fact of the matter is that people as tall&nbsp;
as Garrett don't usually become leading men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:20.640
on TV or in the movies. The average male&nbsp;
lead in Hollywood stands at about 5' 9".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:26.000
So he will probably be recognized as the&nbsp;
record-holder for a very, very long time.

